  "worked_solution": "Let $a(t)=\\frac{40}{(t+1)^{3}}$ be the rate at which the amount of charge stored is changing and let $A(T)$ be the amount of charge stored $T$ seconds after charging begins. \n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nA(T)=A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}a(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(T) & =A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =7+{\\int }_{0}^{T}\\frac{40}{(t+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =7+(-\\frac{20}{(t+1)^{2}}){|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =7+(-\\frac{20}{(T+1)^{2}})-(-\\frac{20}{{1}^{2}}) \\\\  & =7-\\frac{20}{(T+1)^{2}}+20 \\\\  & =27-\\frac{20}{(T+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the amount of charge stored $T=1$ second after charging begins, we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\nA(1)=7-\\frac{20}{(1+1)^{2}}+20=22.\n$$\n\nThus, after $1$ second, the device stores $22$ coulombs of charge.",
